
# Assignment 4 - Customize Your Linux Terminal

## Scenario
Add custom shell functions so every new terminal automatically has a set of productivity commands.

## Where the Code Lives
All functions were added to the end of ~/.bashrc, so they are available automatically in every new terminal session.

## Commands / Functions

### print_content
Displays the contents of the current directory.
Usage: print_content
Implementation: runs ls -l

### go_to <directory>
Changes the current working directory to the specified location.
Usage: go_to ~/Projects
Implementation: runs cd "$1", with an error message if the directory does not exist

### search "keyword" filename
Displays all matching lines in a file, with the matching text highlighted.
Usage: search "hello" sample.txt
Implementation: runs grep --color=always -n "$1" "$2"

### show_file filename
Displays the contents of a file with line numbers.
Usage: show_file sample.txt
Implementation: runs cat -n "$1"

### remove filename
Asks for confirmation before deleting a file.
Usage: remove sample.txt
Implementation: uses read -p to prompt y/n, then runs rm "$1" only if the user confirms with y

### make_dir project/src/include
Creates the complete directory hierarchy, even if intermediate directories do not exist.
Usage: make_dir project/src/include
Implementation: runs mkdir -p "$1"

### create_file notes.txt
Creates an empty file.
Usage: create_file notes.txt
Implementation: runs touch "$1"

## Concepts Used
- Shell functions defined in ~/.bashrc
- Positional parameters ($1, $2)
- Conditionals (if/else) for the delete confirmation
- read -p for interactive user prompts
- Commands: ls, cd, grep, cat, rm, mkdir, touch
